{
  "id": "COMPOUND:17163 - PROTEIN:5836",
  "verbalization": "Norarecoline is implicated in the pathogenesis of HUVEC (human umbilical vein endothelial cell) through a series of mechanistic links. The evidence suggests that Norarecoline targets Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family member 9, a member of the sulfotransferase family, which is involved in the metabolism of various substrates. This target is also engaged by (2E,4E)-N-isobutylundeca-2,4-dien-8,10-diynamide and Erysimoside, both of which are known to interact with HUVEC. The presence of multiple targets within the SDR family, all of which are associated with HUVEC, indicates a potential role in endothelial function or inflammation. The final scores from the paths suggest a consistent and plausible relationship, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these targets highlights the importance of SDR family members in HUVEC biology, and Norarecoline's involvement in this pathway may have implications for vascular health.",
